# Swift/iOS coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' style rules to the Swift API Design Guidelines (Swift 6.x) and SwiftUI idiom.

## Naming and structure

- No `Protocol` suffix on a protocol name; name it as a capability (`Fetchable`)
  or role noun (`DataSource`).
- File name matches the primary type it declares; one primary type per file for
  anything public.

## Value types vs reference types

- Default to `struct` (and `enum` for closed sets) for model and state types.
- `class` only for deliberate reference semantics: shared mutable state across
  owners, identity (`===`), or Objective-C interop; justify a `class` model type in review.
- A `class` used as SwiftUI-observed state is `@Observable` (see `patterns.mdc`)
  and `@MainActor` when view code touches its mutable state.

## Guard for early exit

- Use `guard` with an early `return`/`continue`/`throw` to keep the happy path
  unindented, not an `if let` wrapping the function body.
- One `guard` with comma-separated conditions over a pyramid of nested `if let`.

## Optionals

- `!` and `try!` only for a value whose absence is a programmer error that
  should crash in development (a storyboard `IBOutlet`, a compile-time-guaranteed
  literal); never for network responses, decoded JSON or user input.
- Prefer `guard let`/`if let`, `??` with a real default, or `try`/`try?` with
  explicit handling; in tests use `#require(...)` or `XCTUnwrap` instead of `!`.
- Use `?.` chaining, not a force-unwrapped intermediate step.

## @Observable vs legacy ObservableObject

- For iOS 17+ targets, use `@Observable`, not `ObservableObject`/`@Published`.
- Mark an `@Observable` class `@MainActor` unless the project sets a module-wide
  default actor isolation of `MainActor`.
- Use `ObservableObject`/`@Published`/`@StateObject`/`@ObservedObject` only when
  the minimum deployment target predates iOS 17 or the codebase has not migrated;
  never introduce it in new code on an iOS 17+ project.

## Formatting and linting

- Format with the project's configured formatter (`swift-format` or `swiftformat`);
  do not hand-format around it.
- Fix a SwiftLint finding at its root cause; no `// swiftlint:disable` covering
  more than the one line, and no repository-wide rule disable to clear one finding.
